<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cần Vương</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1885–1896</span> · Also known as Can Vuong movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cần Vương ('Aid the King') movement was a Vietnamese uprising against French rule launched in July 1885, when the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ton-that-thuyet">Tôn Thất Thuyết</a> fled Huế with the boy emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ham-nghi">Hàm Nghi</a> and issued an edict calling on scholars and people to resist. Led by local scholar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>, it fought in central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>; Hàm Nghi was captured in 1888, and the last major leader, Phan Đình Phùng, died in 1895.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/can-vuong.jpg" alt="Cần Vương" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nguyen Dynasty in 19th century · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chieu_Can_Vuong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
